\startsubsection[reference=sec:spectral,title={Spectral decomposition}]

Spectral decomposition is the act of transforming the data into components that each capture a different source of variance in the data.
These components are scored by their ability to explain the data’s structure.
By keeping components with high enough scores as embedding dimensions, spectral decompositions can therefore be used as dimensionality reduction methods.
This is most useful as a preprocessing step to define a less noisy latent space reflecting the inherent dimensionality of the data, but components can also be directly visualized.

\startsubsubsection[title={Principal Component Analysis}]
PCA is a simple spectral decomposition.
Its principal components (PCs) are weighted products of input features (such as transcripts), scored by the variance each PC explains.
As each PC ideally represents one source of variation, data with up to around 4 linearly separable sources of variation can be interpreted by visualizing the corresponding number of PCs.
As mentioned before as an example, while the intrinsic dimensionality of transcriptomic data is low, the number of PCs necessary to faithfully represent all biological processes one might care about is around 50-100 \cite[lenz2016].
Picking a number of PCs in this range, for example using the jackstraw algorithm \cite[chung2015] is a popular a preprocessing step for downstream analysis.

There are a number of related methods that can serve a similar purpose, such as independent component analysis \cite[bell1995] or factor analysis \cite[stephenson1935].
\TODO[I don’t use them but mention them for completeness, is that wrong?]
Furthermore, single cell transcriptomics specific extensions exist, such as ZINB-WaVE \cite[risso2018], which addresses zero inflation and over-dispersion of scRNASeq-data in its dimensionality reduction.
\stopsubsubsection

\startsubsubsection[title={Diffusion Maps}]
Diffusion maps provide another spectral decomposition of data.
Their practicality for data analysis was first shown by \inlinecite[coifman2005].
Compared to some \about[sec:learned-embeddings] like t-SNE \cite[vandermaaten2008], diffusion maps preserve global structures and the pseudo-temporal ordering of cells better \cite[haghverdi2015], and the first diffusion components are often sufficient to directly capture and visualize bifurcation events in differentiating cells.

%https://static-content.springer.com/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fnmeth.3971/MediaObjects/41592_2016_BFnmeth3971_MOESM373_ESM.pdf

A diffusion map $s_{pc}$ has as components $p$ the eigenvectors of a matrix of transition probabilities $T_{cd}$ between cells $c$ and $d ∈ \{1, …, C\} ∖ \{c\}$.
\TODO[I already define s, x, g, p, c above. Is it correct to name the used variables again in the context they’re now being used in?]
The transition probabilities are derived from a diffusion model applied to the cells’ expression profiles $x_{gc}$.
There are several flavors of diffusion models, parametrized over the chosen distance function $dist$ and the kernel width $σ$ (square root of the Gaussian function $Y(c)$’s variance).
\TODO[You underlined Y(c) here, but I define it as the next formula!]

The most useful set of flavors is the ones independent of local density.
These flavors parametrize their Gaussian kernel with a cell-dependent kernel width $σ_c$ set to the local density.
Those flavors’ Gaussian function is:

\placeformula
\startformula
Y_c\left(c'\right) =
	∜{2 \over {π σ_c^2}}
	\exp \left(-{
		\left|\left|c'-c\right|\right|^2
		\over
		σ_c^2
	}\right)
\stopformula

Where $σ_c$ can be estimated from $c$’s $k$th-nearest neighbor $\text{NN}_k(c)$:

\placeformula
\startformula
\widehat{σ_c} = dist(c, \text{NN}_k(c))
\stopformula

Since Gaussian functions are wave functions, the kernel $K(c, d)$ is defined by the interference of two wave functions centered at $c$ and $d$:

\placeformula
\startformula
\startalign
\NC K(c, d) \NC =
	∫_{-∞}^∞ Y_c\left(c'\right) Y_d\left(c'\right) \mathword{d} c'
\NR
\NC \NC =
	√{2 σ_c σ_d \over σ_c^2+σ_d^2}
	\exp \left(-{
		||c-d||^2
		\over
		2\left(σ_c^2+σ_d^2\right)
	}\right)
\NR
\stopalign
\stopformula

With a kernel function defined, the connectivities $W_{cd}$ between cells are defined as follows:

\placeformula
\startformula
W_{cd} = {K(c, d) \over Z(c) Z(d)}
\stopformula

\placeformula
\startformula
Z(c) = ∑_{d ∈ \{1,…,C\}} K(c, d)
\stopformula

More commonly today, $W$ is approximated. One approach is to only define $W_{cd}$ for $d∈\{\text{NN}_k(c)\}$ instead of all $d∈\{1,…,C\}$, another is employed by UMAP, which uses fuzzy simplical sets derived from $σ_c$ instead of discrete sets of nearest neighbors.

In any case, a transition probability matrix $T$ can be obtained by row-normalizing $W$:

\placeformula
\startformula
\tilde{Z}(c) = ∑_{d∈\{1,…,C\}} W_{cd}
\stopformula

The traditional variant $T_{cd}^{\mathword{asym}}$, mentioned for completeness, is defined as:

\placeformula
\startformula
T_{cd}^{\mathword{asym}} = \tilde{Z}(c)^{-1} W_{cd}
\stopformula

The symmetrical variant $T_{cd}^{\mathword{sym}}$ has several advantages, including computational performance and the ability to reuse the sparsity structure of $W$: 

\placeformula
\startformula
T_{cd}^{\mathword{sym}} = √{\tilde{Z}(c)}^{-1} W_{cd} √{\tilde{Z}(y)}^{-1}
\stopformula

During the parallel development of destiny \cite[angerer2016] and diffusion pseudo time \cite[haghverdi2016], I helped shape the efficient flavor of diffusion maps most used today \TODO[data].
I pioneered the use of a symmetrized kNN graph represented as a symmetric sparse matrix, using this kNN information as a density estimate for the locally scaled kernel $K$ and its sparsity structure for efficient calculation of the symmetric transition probability matrix $T$.

\stopsubsubsection

\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[reference=sec:learned-embeddings,title={Learned embeddings}]
% https://nbisweden.github.io/workshop-scRNAseq/slides2019/Dimensionality_reduction_P_Czarnewski_2019.pdf
In order to optimize the embedding for certain use cases, machine learning can be employed to learn an optimal embedding directly or a function to optimally embed the expression profiles.
A common goal is to represent relationships in the data as faithfully as possible in a 2- or 3-dimensional embedding that can be displayed on a screen or paper.

One such machine learning driven method, t-distributed stochastic neighborhood embedding (t-SNE, \inlinecite[vandermaaten2008]), remains the most popular dimensionality reduction method \cite[kobak2019].
The more recent method UMAP \cite[mcinnes2018] works similarly, while representing global structures in the data more faithfully \cite[becht2018].
Moreover it is claimed to be viable as a preprocessing dimensionality reduction method like the aforementioned \about[sec:spectral].
\inlinecite[kobak2019] showed that t-SNE with a data-driven selection of meta-parameters still outperforms UMAP under some circumstances.
\TODO[put the above into relation: can’t UMAP be tuned to be like t-SNE?]

A disadvantages of machine learning based methods is their nondeterministic nature:
the optimization converging to local optima can result in unstable inference of certain cells’ neighborhoods in the embedding.
Another problem is that most common machine learning methods learn an embedding directly instead of an embedding function, making it impossible to project new data into an existing embedding.
There are approaches to remedy this for UMAP and t-SNE \TODO[cite].
\stopsubsection

\stopsection

\startsection[title={Gene relevance}]

\definemathcommand[lr][nolop]{\mfunction{LR}}
\definemathcommand[gr][nolop]{\mfunction{GR}}
\definemathcommand[rank][nolop]{\mfunction{rank}}

Due to their lesser expressivity, basic dimensionality reduction methods based on linear combinations like PCA are rarely used for visualizing scRNA-Seq data anymore.
The nonlinear methods used instead however lack the linear methods’ intrinsic relation from reduced dimensions to input genes.
We developed the gene relevance method in order to derive this relation from scRNA-Seq data and a given embedding \cite[angerer2020].
The method defines a gene relevance score $R_{gc}$, as well as summary scores called local gene relevance $\lr_r(g, Ψ)$ and global gene relevance $\gr_r(g)$.
\TODO[mention GR map ($l^m_c$) as well?]
The gene relevance score is a measure of the extent in which a gene drives the embedding coordinates of a specific cell.
It can be derived from the gradient $∇_{gc}$ of the function mapping a embedding $s_{pc}$ to the gene expression $x_{gc}$:
\TODO[∇f is the gradient of the function f, and I think that’s in our case the function mapping the embedding to the gene expressions, so the inverse of the embedding function. Does that make sense?]

% https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nabla-Operator
% use the ∇ operator like a vector, where the “product” of e.g. $∂ / ∂x_i$ with a function f on the right is interpreted as the partial derivative $∂f / ∂x_i$. 

\placeformula
\startformula
∇_{gc} = \left(\frac{∂ x_{gc}}{∂ s_{1c}}, …, \frac{∂ x_{gc}}{∂ s_{Pc}}\right)
\stopformula

This gradient can be estimated using local differences.
\TODO[is it called that?]
To address zero inflation, the gradient is only defined for $x_{gc}≠0$:
\TODO[introduce zero inflation in intro]

\placeformula
\startformula
\widehat{∇_{gc}} = \left(
\startcases[left={\left.}, style=\displaystyle]
\NC \mathit{NA}, \NC \text{if } x_{gc} = 0 \NR
\NC \underset{n ∈ \text{NN}_k(c) ∧ n≠c}\median \frac{x_{gc}-x_{gn}}{s_{pn}-s_{pc}}, \NC \text{otherwise} \NR
\stopcases
\right)
\stopformula

The gene relevance score $R_{gc}$ is defined as the euclidean norm of this gradient:

\placeformula
\startformula
R_{gc} = \left|\left|∇_{gc}\right|\right|_2 = \sqrt{∑_{p=1}^P \left(∇_{gc}\right)_p^2}
\stopformula

Local gene relevance $\lr_r(g, Ψ)$ is a measure of how much a gene $g$ is driving the embedding given a set of cells $Ψ⊆\{1,…,C\}$.
Local gene relevance is defined depending on a rank cutoff $r∈\{2,…,|Ψ|\}$.

\placeformula
\startformula
\lr_r\left(g, Ψ\right) =
	\frac1{\left|Ψ\right|}
	∑_{c∈Ψ ∧ x_{gc}≠0} \left[ \rank\left(R_{gc}\right) < r \right]
\stopformula

With the \inlinecite[iverson1962] bracket notation:

\placeformula
\startformula
\left[P\right] =
\startcases
\NC 1, \NC \text{if $P$ is true} \NR
\NC 0, \NC \text{otherwise} \NR
\stopcases
\text{, for any predicate $P$}
\stopformula

The set of cells of interest $Ψ$ can be obtained by metadata such as experimental markers or the labels from a clustering algorithm.
Another alternative is the visualization of local relevance by dividing the embedding into shaped bins, which can be colored according to their respective local relevance.

Global gene relevance $\gr_r(g)$ is the local relevance of the set of all genes $\{1,…,C\}$:

\placeformula
\startformula
\gr_r\left(g\right) = \lr_r(g, \{1,…,C\})
\stopformula

Gene relevance maps label each cell with the gene having the highest gene relevance score for that cell.
Because this labeling is subject to noise, $m$ smoothing steps over the scores of each cells’ neighborhood are employed to obtain less and more stable labels.

\placeformula
\startformula
l_c^m = \argmax_{g∈Ω ∧ x_{gc}≠0} 
\startcases[style=\displaystyle]
\NC R_{gc}, \NC \text{if $m=0$} \NR
\NC {\textstyle \frac1k} ∑_{n ∈ \text{NN}_k(c)} \left[ l_n^{m-1} = g \right], \NC \text{otherwise} \NR
\stopcases
\stopformula

Gene relevance is robust towards its rank cutoff parameter $r$ and reasonably robust towards its smoothing parameter $m$ \cite[angerer2020], given that strong smoothing $m≥3$ often results in all cells sharing the single most globally relevant gene as label.
The implementation is sufficiently efficient in terms of computational time and memory that quick iteration is possible.

I developed and implemented all of the mentioned concepts as part of destiny.
The implementation offers a rich and user friendly API including plotting routines for all concepts.

\stopsection

\startsection[title={Scanpy and AnnData}]

\TODO[Efficiency (PCA, sparse, …) and APIs]
Scanpy is a Python library providing functions and classes for scRNA-Seq preprocessing, analysis, and visualization.
It is designed and developed in tandem with AnnData, a general purpose data structure for homogeneous, annotated two dimensional data (see \infig[fig:anndata]).
AnnData’s structure matches the annotated observations-by-features ($c×g$) matrix format most biological snapshot data is analyzed in.
For example in single cell imaging, such a matrix can be constructed containing each segmented cell $c$’s average fluorescent channel $g$ value.
Further examples include data from RNA-Seq or quantitative real-time polymerase chain reaction (qRT-PCR, \inlinecite[nurmi2002]), where $g$ stands for gene expression.
In bulk protocols, $c$ is a tissue sample, and in single cell protocols $c$ is a cell.
In any case, further information per sample or feature can exist, for example from other experiments or by classifying cells according to shape or other characteristics that are not represented directly in $g$ or $c$.

The central element of each AnnData object \type{adata} is consequently a stack of named layers \type{adata.layers}, each holding all features $g$ measured for all observations $c$.
\footnote{Missing observations can be represented by the special pseudo-value \emph{NA}.}
The default layer can be accessed via \type{adata.X}.
For each observation and feature, an unique name is stored by which it can be addressed (\type{adata.obs_names}, \type{adata.var_names}).
Each layer has one homogeneous scalar type able to represent it in sufficient IEEE 754 floating-point precision or integer size.
The layers can be dense Numpy arrays \cite[vanderwalt2011] for normalized or densely measured data or Scipy sparse matrices \cite[virtanen2020] for zero-inflated scRNA-Seq data.
\TODO[mention zero inflation in intro]
Named one dimensional annotations such as cluster labels, gene IDs alternative to the feature name, or ranking indices are stored in their respective AnnData dimension’s Pandas data frame \cite[mckinney2010] (\type{adata.obs}, \type{adata.var}).
\TODO[mention clustering in intro]
Further, AnnData stores named multidimensional annotations (\type{adata.obsm}, \type{adata.varm}) and square sparse matrices representing weighted graphs (\type{adata.obsp}, \type{adata.varp}), each aligned to an AnnData dimension.
AnnData maintains relations between data and annotations, keeping them in sync through filtering and concatenation.
Due to the generic structure, Scanpy and other tools can interact by adding and reading annotations, as well as reading, modifying and adding layers.

Scanpy’s design is structured around ease of use without compromising performance.
This allows analyzing of up to a million cells in reasonable time in a familiar interactive environment.
\TODO[Jupyter]

