\startsection[title={BART-Seq (demultiplexing and quantification)}]

\TODO[methods should be self contained. repeat intro with mre details if necessary]
BART-Seq is a targeted RNA sequencing protocol able to sensitively and inexpensively measure expressions of thousands of samples per experiment, such as single cells or cell groups \cite[uzbas2019].
It consists of three steps; design, experiment, and quantification.

% primer/barcode design
The design step involves algorithms to design primer pairs matching amplicons – such as genes – and barcodes identifying samples.
The primer design algorithm optimizes primer pairs to stably dimerize with their target amplicons.
Each pair contains two primers binding to the 3' ends of each strand of the target amplicon, forward and reverse.
\TODO[check if 3' or 5']
The set of primer pairs is split up into one set of forward and one of reverse primers.
The barcode design algorithm minimizes the chance of misassigning barcodes through sequencing errors by maximizing their mutual alignment distances.
The set of barcodes is split into two halves arbitrarily, one half per primer set.
The results are the sequences for a forward and reverse set of barcodes, a forward and reverse linker, and a forward and reverse primer per amplicon.

% experiment
For the experimental step, all barcode-linker and reverted complement (r.\,c.) primer-linker sequences are synthesized in sufficient quantities.
Separately for each barcode, all combinations of forward primers and forwards barcodes are created by annealing barcode-linker and r.\,c. primer-linker strands arbitrarily, filling in the resulting incomplete double strands, and removing the r.\,c. strands.
Analogously all possible reverse barcode-linker-primer strands are synthesized per reverse barcode.
Each well of a sequencing plate receives a different combination of forward and reverse barcode-linker-primer assemblies, allowing to address $(\frac12\text{\#barcodes})^2$ wells.
The experiment concludes like a regular paired-end sequencing run \cite[fullwood2009], resulting in a raw read library containing strands of barcode-linker-read.

% quantification: sequencing and multiplexing
In the quantification step, the barcodes, linkers and low-quality bases are cut off the raw read sequence.
The shortened read sequences are then tagged with their corresponding designed barcode if possible.
This process assigns barcodes with up to one mismatching base using the algorithm of \inlinecite[aho1975], as long as the assignment is still unique – reads that could be tagged with one of multiple barcodes that are one mismatch away from each other are discarded.
The tagged reads are then aligned to the targeted amplicons using HISAT2 \cite[kim2019].
Amplicons are counted per barcode combination according to one of two strategies.
The conservative strategy only counts where both reads aligned to the same amplicon, while the sensitive strategy also counts when one of both is unaligned.
Neither strategy counts one of the aligned amplicons when there is a mismatch.
The count of overall full matches, half matches, and mismatches is included in the various statistics collected about the demultiplexing and quantification process.

I designed the quantification step in collaboration with Fatma Uzbas and Micha Drucker, and implemented a Python library and pipeline executing it.

